## Question 3: Generate the Next 7 Days Using a Recursive CTE

### Sample Table Structure: CalendarDays

| Column | Data Type |
|---|---|
| day_date | DATE |

This question generates dates using a recursive CTE, so no existing sample records are required.

### SQL Query (MySQL 8.0+)

```sql
WITH RECURSIVE CalendarDays AS (
    SELECT CURRENT_DATE AS day_date

    UNION ALL

    SELECT DATE_ADD(day_date, INTERVAL 1 DAY)
    FROM CalendarDays
    WHERE day_date < DATE_ADD(CURRENT_DATE, INTERVAL 6 DAY)
)
SELECT day_date
FROM CalendarDays;
```

### Sample Result Output

*Example assuming today is 2026-10-27.*

| day_date |
|---|
| 2026-10-27 |
| 2026-10-28 |
| 2026-10-29 |
| 2026-10-30 |
| 2026-10-31 |
| 2026-11-01 |
| 2026-11-02 |

**Explanation:** The recursive CTE begins with the current date and adds one day in each step. It produces seven dates, including today and the following six days. The actual output changes according to the date on which the query runs.

